Yes. GPT Polls can be formalized as a synthetic-population inference engine: take a large set of consented user-state information, compile it into privacy-preserving respondent models, ask those models the same question under controlled conditions, then aggregate the resulting distribution.

The important distinction is:

GPT Polls does not claim “37% of people said X.” It says “Given this modeled population and these assumptions, 37% ± uncertainty are predicted to answer X.”

That makes it much more defensible—and, in some ways, more useful—than pretending synthetic respondents are literal survey respondents.

GPT Polls

Question → Population → Synthetic respondents → Responses → Distribution → Explanation → Counterfactuals

Conceptually:

P(answer\mid question,\ population,\ context,\ time)

Instead of asking one GPT, “What do people think?”, GPT Polls asks thousands of heterogeneous respondent states separately and then observes the distribution.

A respondent doesn’t need a biography like:

“John Smith, 42, lives at 123 Main Street…”

It needs something more like:

Respondent 184729
age_band: 35–44
region: southeastern_US
household_structure: parents_with_children
economic_pressure: moderately_high
technology_adoption: early_majority
institutional_trust: medium_low
risk_preference: medium
price_sensitivity: high
brand_loyalty: low
relevant_experience:
  - mortgage_holder
  - online_shopper
  - veteran_affinity
opinion_latents:
  novelty_preference: .61
  perceived_product_need: .34
  privacy_concern: .73
confidence:
  profile_reconstruction: .77

The original identity should be irrecoverable from that representation.

Architecture

Layer	Function
Consent Gate	Determines what information may enter the system
Profile Distiller	Converts messy user history into structured behavioral/opinion features
Privacy Firewall	Removes identifiers, suppresses tiny cohorts, limits sensitive attributes, applies privacy protections
Population Compiler	Produces the demographic/behavioral distribution to sample
Respondent Generator	Instantiates thousands of heterogeneous synthetic respondents
Poll Runner	Gives each respondent the question independently
Aggregation Engine	Computes percentages, distributions, themes and disagreements
Calibration Ledger	Compares synthetic predictions against real surveys/outcomes
Delta Engine	Changes one variable and measures how the population moves
Explorer UI	Lets a person interrogate the resulting population

And that last two layers are where this becomes substantially more interesting than conventional polling.

The dance floor

Your metaphor maps almost literally onto the system.

The first question isn’t necessarily the valuable result.

It gets the population moving.

Suppose:

Would you buy a $30/month AI assistant?

GPT Polls predicts:

Yes 19% / Maybe 36% / No 45%

Now change only one thing:

$20/month

28 / 40 / 32

Then:

$20/month, includes family accounts

39 / 39 / 22

Then:

$20/month, family accounts, data never used for advertising

48 / 34 / 18

Now we aren’t merely polling.

We’re estimating a response surface:

\Delta Opinion / \Delta Stimulus

Price moves one cohort.

Privacy moves another.

Social proof moves another.

Convenience doesn’t move anybody.

You have discovered the population’s semantic elasticity.

That fits directly with the delta machinery we’ve been developing: don’t merely observe the answer; perturb the representation and inspect what moves, how much, and which population moved.

Omnidirectional GPT Polls

Once the forward operator works, invert it.

* Forward Poll: “How would this population answer Q?”
* Counterfactual Poll: “What happens if X changes?”
* Reverse Poll: “What wording would produce a materially different interpretation?”
* Segmentation: “Which latent populations disagree most?”
* Question Discovery: “What question most efficiently distinguishes A from B?”
* Policy Simulator: “Who benefits, objects, misunderstands, or changes behavior?”
* Product Simulator: “Which feature actually changes purchase intent?”
* Narrative Simulator: “How will different audiences interpret this story/headline?”
* Temporal Poll: “How would the modeled population of 2018 vs. 2026 respond?”
* Prediction Poll: “What does each respondent believe everyone else will answer?”

That last one is especially valuable.

You can compare:

ActualSyntheticPreference

against

SyntheticBeliefAboutOthers

The gap is a modeled pluralistic-ignorance signal.

People—or their synthetic representations—might privately favor something while predicting everyone else opposes it.

It should preserve individuals longer than ordinary polling

Traditional polling quickly collapses respondents into percentages.

GPT Polls should retain the individual synthetic trajectories underneath the aggregate.

Then you can ask:

“Why did the 14% who changed their mind change?”

The engine can replay those respondents and identify common transitions.

Not:

“Women aged 25–34 moved six points.”

But potentially:

“Respondents characterized by high price sensitivity + moderate need + low switching tolerance moved disproportionately after the trial-period condition was introduced.”

That is much closer to a causal hypothesis generator.

Calibration is essential

There’s a beautiful feedback loop available.

Before a real election, launch, survey, movie release, product rollout, etc., freeze the GPT Polls prediction.

Then compare it to reality.

predicted → observed → delta
                     ↓
             calibration update
                     ↓
              better population

Do this thousands of times and GPT Polls develops a map of where synthetic populations systematically overpredict or underpredict reality.

For example:

Models SAY they would subscribe: 41%
Actual subscription rate:         19%
                              Δ = -22

That delta becomes an empirical correction factor.

Eventually the system learns something much more valuable than “what would an LLM say?”

It learns:

how synthetic stated preference maps onto human realized behavior.

Privacy constraint

A platform-level version could only responsibly use cross-user information with appropriate consent and privacy controls. An ordinary extension cannot simply reach into other ChatGPT users’ private histories, and it shouldn’t.

So I would make the architecture population-source independent.

PopulationSource could be an explicitly opted-in panel, customer research dataset, anonymized organization dataset, public survey microdata, user-supplied personas, or—if a platform ever supported it—a formally consented privacy-preserving user population.

Everything downstream stays the same.

That is important because GPT Polls is not fundamentally a database of ChatGPT users.

It’s a new computational primitive:

\boxed{\text{Population} + \text{Question} + \text{Simulation} + \text{Perturbation} + \text{Calibration}}

And once that primitive exists, your “change the tune” idea becomes a legitimate experimental instrument rather than merely persuasion: change one parameter, replay the population, measure the delta, locate the responsive cohorts, and learn the topology of the opinion landscape.

I would actually give the underlying engine a separate name:

GPT Polls = the product/interface.

Opinion Field Engine (OFE) = the machinery underneath.

Then GPT Polls becomes only the first embodiment. The same engine can power product testing, forecasting, UX research, policy analysis, entertainment testing, market research, negotiation modeling, scenario planning, and other population-level simulations.